58da-94fa-691c00244659" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434770 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434771 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434772 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434778 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434780 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1077 "selection" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434770 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434784 1079 #uuid "40038cf6-6965-5518-9d52-0ed4bca4de62" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434785 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434776 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434785 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434785 1077 "field-1" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434785 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434776 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434785 1079 #uuid "645dd210-943e-5360-a7a2-cfed7ab075c6" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434783 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434786 1069 "q-277952" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434786 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x(4{x}^{2}+7)}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434786 1072 #uuid "030d4922-51e9-524a-b5f1-bb14227873b0" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434786 1074 "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{12{x}^{3}+21x}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{12{x}^{3}+21x}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{21x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12+\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{5}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(12+\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{12+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434782 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434787 1069 "q-6994" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434787 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+5)}{17x+7}.$" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434787 1072 #uuid "240f8824-faa1-5120-9751-29976f966dc7" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434787 1074 "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{17x}{x}+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{10}{x}$ and $\\frac{7}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3-x}{17+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x+3}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434784 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434788 1069 "q-6885" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434788 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434788 1072 #uuid "6ea2595d-82f6-56e5-b377-9d57e3c87c0f" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434788 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434785 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434789 1069 "q-277948" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434789 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7x-9{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+8x-5}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434789 1072 #uuid "eabf075e-fdbb-5a7f-b63d-7f2c28ee58cc" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true] [17592186434789 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x-9{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+8x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-9)}{(3+\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},$ $\\frac{8}{x},$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-9)}{(3+\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-9}{3+0-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$" 13194139534742 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-14c1c570-e286-5d61-a7d1-a10aa9c6de44" 17592186434770 "answer-23025a8b-6786-567b-bd9a-eb8e19beee8c" 17592186434771 "answer-2c78aa54-0ac9-53df-b432-2d5fc7834c15" 17592186434772 "answer-2fea007a-0dd4-5f6c-9257-ed12e9d45202" 17592186434773 "answer-3576ce64-1716-532c-9b43-4e79dd447ff6" 17592186434774 "answer-50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" 17592186434775 "answer-5bea34e5-3538-5b53-9d1b-3da84e003295" 17592186434776 "answer-81a8f7bc-4cfa-55bc-a03d-5635c11ef30b" 17592186434777 "answer-934b0a62-a4f3-5bdb-bdb5-7947c45a8